s because the place values increase by a factor of 10 as we move from right to left. For example, in the units place, the value of a digit is 1, in the tens place it's 10, in the hundreds place it's 100, and so on. \n\nWhen the sum of the digits in a column exceeds the value of that place (for example, 10 in the units place), we cannot represent this sum as a single digit. Instead, we write down the units digit of the sum and carry over the tens digit to the next column to the left. This carried-over digit is then added to the sum of the next column.\n\nFor instance, in the addition of 787576373 and 796585110, when we add the digits in the ten millions place, we get 8 + 9 = 17. We write down the 7 and carry over the 1 to the next column to the left. This carried-over 1 is then added to the sum of the next column, ensuring that we account for the full value of each place.\n\nThis process of carrying over is crucial in maintaining the correct place value and ensuring the accuracy of the addition.\n</think>\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\nSure! Let's break down why we carry over when the sum of digits in a column is 10 or more.\n\nWhen we add two numbers, each digit in the sum represents a specific place value. For example, in the number 787576373, the digit 7 in the ten millions place represents 70,000,000, and the digit 7 in the millions place represents 7,000,000. When we add the digits in a column, if the sum exceeds the value of that place, we need to carry over the tens digit to the next column to the left.\n\nLet's illustrate this with the addition of 787576373 and 796585110:\n\n```\n  787576373\n+ 796585110\n-----------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n- 3 + 0 = 3\n- 7 + 1 = 8\n- 6 + 1 = 7\n- 3 + 5 = 8\n- 7 + 8 = 15 (write down 5, carry over 1)\n- 5 + 6 + 1 (carried over) = 12 (write down 2, carry over 1)\n- 7 + 5 + 1 (carried over) = 13 (write down 3, carry over 1)\n- 8 + 9 + 1 (carried over) = 18 (write down 8, carry over 1)\n- 7 + 7 + 1 (carried over) = 15 (write down 5, carry over 1)\n- 7 + 9 + 1 (carried over) = 17 (write down 7, carry over 1)\n- 1 + 0 + 1 (carried over) = 2\n\nThe carried-over digits are added to the next column to the left. For instance, in the ten millions place, we have 8 + 9 = 17. We write down the 7 and carry over the 1 to the next column. This carried-over 1 is then added to the sum of the next column, ensuring that we account for the full value of each place.\n\nThis process of carrying over is essential to maintain the correct place value and ensure the accuracy of the addition. It prevents us from underestimating the value of each place and ensures that the sum is correctly calculated.\n</answer>",
