 A:**\n    - Initial Inventory: 100 units\n    - Sales: 50 units\n    - Purchases: 30 units\n    - Remaining Inventory: 80 units (100 - 50 + 30)\n  - **Shortage of Item B:**\n    - Initial Inventory: 200 units\n    - Sales: 100 units\n    - Purchases: 50 units\n    - Remaining Inventory: 150 units (200 - 100 + 50)\n  - **Shortage of Item C:**\n    - Initial Inventory: 150 units\n    - Sales: 75 units\n    - Purchases: 20 units\n    - Remaining Inventory: 195 units (150 - 75 + 20)\n\n**Scenario with Carrying Over:**\n\nNow, let's consider an inventory shortage that requires carrying over the shortage to the next period.\n\n**Scenario 1: Inventory Shortage**\n- **Shortage of Item A:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 80 units (100 - 50 + 30)\n- **Shortage of Item B:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 150 units (200 - 100 + 50)\n- **Shortage of Item C:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 195 units (150 - 75 + 20)\n\n**Scenario 2: Adjusting for the Shortage**\n- **Shortage of Item A:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 80 units (100 - 50 + 30)\n- **Shortage of Item B:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 150 units (200 - 100 + 50)\n- **Shortage of Item C:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 195 units (150 - 75 + 20)\n\n**Scenario 3: Carrying Over the Shortage**\n- **Shortage of Item A:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 80 units (100 - 50 + 30)\n- **Shortage of Item B:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 150 units (200 - 100 + 50)\n- **Shortage of Item C:**\n  - Remaining Inventory: 195 units (150 - 75 + 20)\n\n**Real-World Application:**\n\nIn this scenario, carrying over is crucial because it ensures that inventory shortages are accurately accounted for. If you don't carry over the shortage to the next period, you might not be able to replenish the inventory in a timely manner, leading to stockouts and potential customer dissatisfaction.\n\nThis example illustrates how carrying over is not just a theoretical concept but a practical tool used in inventory management. If you have any more questions or need further clarification, f